# A row-split prompt chunk's DSA layers run their replicated front on each rank's own rows (TF_GLM_PROMPT_OWN_FRONT=1
# with TF_GLM_HC_SPLIT=1; off by default until measured on the Sparks):
#
# - Why: with the row split each rank ends a layer holding the next layer's normed input for its own rows only and
#   swapped them (hidden 4,096 x bf16: 8 KiB a row), so that every rank ran the next block on every row. KDA and the
#   MoE need every row (split by heads and experts), but most of a DSA block's front is replicated: q_a / kv_a and their
#   norms, the indexer's key / weight projection, its gate, its query projection and the top-k over every visible
#   pooled key, which grows with the context. Every rank ran all of it on every row.
# - own_front.front: before a DSA layer the glue keeps the normed rows on their owner (HcSplit.glue(share=False)); each
#   rank runs the front on its own rows (forward.dsa_front, already run during the glue with TF_GLM_PREFILL_OVERLAP=2,
#   plus the indexer's keys, weights, gates and queries) and shares the outputs: the index keys / weights (bf16) and
#   gates (fp32), then the q_a / kv_a norms' rows (4 KiB a row); every rank writes every row's index keys, gates and
#   pooled keys, selects its own rows' top-512 pools (sparse.select_pools) and shares them (512 int32 a row); every
#   rank then makes every row's tokens from the pools (sparse.pool_tokens, select_tokens' formulas). own_front.rest:
#   q_b for the rank's heads on the other rows, the latent write, absorb, dense attention, sparse attention, expand and
#   o_proj, as before.
# - hcsplit: HcSplit.share / wait (whole own shares, send/receive or the all-gather, on the side stream with the
#   overlap) and glue(share=False); SplitSettings.own_front joins the ranks' startup comparison.
# - sparse: _select_prompt's blocks factored into _prompt_blocks, select_pools and pool_tokens (the same kernels).
# - forward: the prompt's sparse attention call in one place (sparse_prompt), used by dsa_rows and own_front.
# - Kept on the unchanged path: several streams' prompts in one chunk (multi_prefill's mixer), chunks too short to
#   split, decode and verify windows, TF_GLM_LATENT=0.
#
# Bits: every kernel is the unchanged path's on the same row; a row's pools depend on its query, weights and the
# pooled keys at or before its position, written on every rank before any rank selects, and are the same in any block
# of rows; partials are still summed rank 0 first; every rank writes its caches from the same shared bytes. Rows that
# keep dense attention (count 0) may get other, unread, pools.
#
# Bytes a DSA layer's own rows send (instead of 8 KiB a row of normed rows): 4.8 KiB a row (index keys 320 B, gates
# 512 B, the norms' rows 4,096 B), 6.8 KiB once the chunk has sparse rows (pools 2 KiB).
#
# GPU check (one GB10, a 2,048-row chunk, FP8 and bf16 pooled keys, 2.6k to 128k tokens): each rank's own-row pools at
# TP=2 / 3 / 4 give exactly the whole chunk's tokens and counts. The selection a rank runs at 128k (FP8): 26.8 ms on
# 2,048 rows, 13.6 / 8.7 / 6.3 ms on its 1,024 / 683 / 512 (32k: 6.0 -> 2.8 / 2.0 / 1.4 ms).
#
# Provenance and credit: ported from the GLM-5.3 recipe's patch 0147 (Mia's AI Lab) to Flash's DSA (pooled keys, no
# rotary part; pools shared instead of token lists). The design (the replicated projections and the indexer's top-k on
# a rank's own rows, their outputs gathered) follows drowzeys' sequence-parallel prompt path, which drowzeys authored:
# github.com/drowzeys/TensorFold, branch glm53-tp4-2026-10-05, compute_prompt_sp with TF_GLM53_SP_SELECT. No code was
# copied from it.
